{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Summary\n\nThe deployed `rebar` **MCP server**'s `review_plan` cannot run its LLM passes: the container\nhas no AWS Bedrock provider package, while the configured model is a Bedrock model. The review\ndegrades to the deterministic floor and returns an unsigned `INDETERMINATE`, so **no\nplan-review attestation is minted** and the claim gate cannot be satisfied through the MCP\nsurface — even though the repo `.venv` has the provider and the local CLI reviews fine.\n\nObserved via a live `review_plan` call on 2026-08-27 (on story `6f74-2795-5ad4-4a56`):\n\n```\n\"verdict\": \"INDETERMINATE\",\n\"coverage\": { \"llm_ran\": false, \"llm_unavailable\": true,\n  \"llm_error\": \"a bedrock model/provider is configured but the optional bedrock provider\n   package is not installed: pip install 'pydantic-ai-slim[bedrock]'\" },\n\"signature\": { \"signed\": false, \"reason\": \"INDETERMINATE\" }\n```\n\nExpected: the four-pass LLM review runs and, on a non-blocking PASS, signs a plan-review\nattestation — the behaviour `docs/adr/0104-mcp-on-box.md:98` designs this server for (\"the two\nop-cert kinds minted via MCP: plan-review at claim, completion-verifier at close\").\n\n## Root cause — PROVEN, and NOT the originally filed hypothesis\n\nThe filed hypothesis was \"the server runs from a stale/global interpreter lacking the\n`[agents]` Bedrock extra\". Disproved: there is no stale env, and `[agents]` has never carried\nBedrock. The server runs from the compose-built container, whose dependency set is exactly what\n`Dockerfile.mcp` specifies — and that specification omits the configured provider.\n\n- `pyproject.toml:131` — `[agents]` is `pydantic-ai-slim[anthropic,retries,duckduckgo]`; no bedrock.\n- `pyproject.toml:142-144` — `[bedrock]` is a **separate opt-in** extra, deliberately split so\n  boto3 stays out of the lean default install (ticket sporadic-bratty-porcupine).\n- `pyproject.toml:213+` — `[dev]` carries boto3, which is why the repo `.venv` works and\n  masked this in every local session.\n- `infra/compose/Dockerfile.mcp:43` — the image builds with\n  `uv sync --locked --no-dev --extra agents --extra mcp`. No `--extra bedrock`, and `--no-dev`,\n  so neither source of the provider is present.\n- `rebar.toml:198-199` — configured model is `bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-opus-4-8`, and the\n  container's `REBAR_ROOT` roots config discovery at a clone carrying that same file.\n- `src/rebar/llm/bedrock_model.py:127-139` raises the named `LLMConfigError` on the lazy import.\n\nDiscriminating runtime experiment (full detail in the RCA comment): two interpreters, identical\ncode and identical `LLMConfig`, differing only in installed extras. `[agents,mcp]` raises the\nreported error byte-for-byte; `[dev]` builds a `BedrockProvider`. This rules out\nconfig-not-discovered, missing credentials, missing region, and model-string parsing.\n\nAttributed `caused_by` **fd4a-4e91-f179-4932**, the change that introduced `Dockerfile.mcp`\nthree weeks AFTER the Bedrock config already existed. Close class `plan_defect`.\n\n## Second gap on the same surface (the fix fails differently without it)\n\nThe `mcp` compose service and `mcp_run_new` in `autodeploy.sh` set **no AWS region**, while the\nsibling `review-bot` service sets two, with a MEASURED comment (`docker-compose.yml`, ticket\na574) that in-container `boto3.session.Session().region_name` is `None` and bedrock-runtime\nraises `NoRegionError` — independently of IMDS, which resolves. Credentials themselves are\nfine: instance `i-00880b2c7f13527c5` (us-east-1) carries role `rebar-gerrit-instance-role`\nwith an inline `bedrock:InvokeModel` policy on `anthropic.claude-*`.\n\n## Fix plan\n\n**1. `infra/compose/Dockerfile.mcp`** — add `--extra bedrock` to the locked install. Keep\n`[agents]` and `[bedrock]` separate rather than flattening: the split is deliberate, and\n`[agents]` is what every PyPI consumer of `nava-rebar` installs, so folding boto3 in would tax\nevery non-Bedrock adopter to fix one deployment. The lock already covers it (`uv sync --locked\n--no-dev --extra agents --extra mcp --extra bedrock --dry-run` exits 0).\n\n**2. `infra/compose/docker-compose.yml`** (`mcp` service) — add `REBAR_LLM_BEDROCK_REGION` and\n`AWS_DEFAULT_REGION`, both `us-east-1`, matching the review-bot's already-proven posture. The\ntwo are not interchangeable: rebar's knob threads into `BedrockProvider` and is recorded in the\nsigned verdict's provenance; `AWS_DEFAULT_REGION` is what a bare boto3 caller reads.\n\n**3. `infra/scripts/autodeploy.sh`** (`mcp_run_new`) — the same two `-e` flags. This is the path\na real deploy takes, so compose-only vars would never reach a container.\n\n**4. `tests/unit/test_mcp_on_box_infra.py`** — RED-first guards, in that file's existing\nimage/compose-contract style: a **coupling** guard that parses `rebar.toml`'s configured\nproviders (via rebar's own `infer_provider`, so it cannot drift from the resolver) and asserts\nthe image installs an extra able to serve each; plus region parity for compose and autodeploy.\n\n## Explicitly OUT of scope — operator decision, reported not changed\n\n`rebar.toml:209-210` gives the `frontier` and `standard` classes direct-`anthropic:` fallbacks,\nand `should_fall_back` (`src/rebar/llm/model_classes.py:366-393`) uses them on an availability\nerror; the container also receives a real `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` via `.env`. So a non-Bedrock\nprovider is reachable. Not removed here: `rebar.toml:181-187` records an explicit operator\ndecision (2026-08-07, ticket 2876) that this arm is approved, and the table is repo-wide, so\nediting it changes local dev and CI too. Making the deployed server Bedrock-only is a scope\ndecision, not a bug fix.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `infra/compose/Dockerfile.mcp` installs the `bedrock` extra alongside `agents` and `mcp`,\n      and the committed lock resolves that set (`uv sync --locked … --dry-run` exits 0).\n- [ ] The `mcp` compose service and `mcp_run_new` each set `REBAR_LLM_BEDROCK_REGION` and\n      `AWS_DEFAULT_REGION` to the instance's region, and the two agree.\n- [ ] A test in `tests/unit/test_mcp_on_box_infra.py` fails when the image's extras cannot\n      serve every provider configured in `rebar.toml`, and passes once the extra is added;\n      shown RED before the fix and killed by re-removing the extra (mutation).\n- [ ] Tests assert region parity between the compose `mcp` service and `mcp_run_new`.\n- [ ] `make lint`, `make typecheck`, `tests/unit/test_mcp_on_box_infra.py` and\n      `tests/unit/test_module_size_contract.py` all pass.\n- [ ] The recurrence guard is recorded where it is enforced, not only in prose: the image's\n      extras are coupled to the configured providers by an executable test, and\n      `Dockerfile.mcp` states why the bedrock extra is required.\n- [ ] LIVE VALIDATION (requires the operator to land and deploy): `review_plan` through the\n      deployed MCP server returns `coverage.llm_ran = true` with no `llm_unavailable`, and on a\n      non-blocking PASS `signature.signed = true`. NOTE the criterion originally proposed here\n      (`find_spec('pydantic_ai.models.bedrock')`) is a WEAK oracle — measured, that module ships\n      with `pydantic-ai-slim` unconditionally and is truthy even with `boto3` absent, so it\n      would have passed on the broken container. Probe `boto3`, or import the provider."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787868757446819001,"uuid":"9476499c-26df-45f5-9652-dc369902be15"}